---
/**
 * Weekly Meal Prep Calculator Tool Page
 *
 * Interactive tool for planning weekly meal prep.
 */

import BaseLayout from '../../components/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MealPrepCalculator from '../../components/tools/MealPrepCalculator';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Weekly Meal Prep Calculator"
  description="Plan your weekly meal prep with our interactive calculator. Organize meals, generate shopping lists, and calculate total prep time for efficient batch cooking."
  keywords={['meal prep calculator', 'weekly meal planning', 'batch cooking planner', 'meal prep schedule', 'food prep organization']}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Tools', url: '/tools' },
    { name: 'Weekly Meal Prep Calculator', url: '/tools/meal-prep' }
  ]}
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="py-12 md:py-16 bg-gradient-to-b from-[var(--color-parchment)] to-[var(--color-cream)]">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto mb-10">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <nav class="flex items-center justify-center gap-2 text-sm text-[var(--text-muted)] mb-6">
          <a href="/" class="hover:text-[var(--color-wine)] transition-colors">Home</a>
          <span>/</span>
          <a href="/tools" class="hover:text-[var(--color-wine)] transition-colors">Tools</a>
          <span>/</span>
          <span class="text-[var(--color-wine)]">Meal Prep Calculator</span>
        </nav>

        <!-- Title -->
        <h1 class="text-display text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
          Weekly <span class="text-accent text-[var(--color-wine)]">Meal Prep</span> Calculator
        </h1>

        <!-- Description -->
        <p class="text-lg text-[var(--text-secondary)] max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          Plan your entire week of meals, calculate total prep time, and generate a
          complete shopping list. Spend one day prepping, eat well all week.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Tool Section -->
  <section class="py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="container">
      <MealPrepCalculator client:load />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Educational Content for SEO -->
  <section class="py-16 bg-white">
    <div class="container max-w-4xl">
      <hr class="divider mb-12" />

      <article class="prose prose-lg">
        <h2 class="text-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-6">
          The Art of Meal Prepping
        </h2>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-10">
          <div class="bg-[var(--color-cream)] rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-[var(--text-primary)]">
              Batching Is the Whole Method
            </h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed">
              One session works because the tasks share heat and equipment: a single oven run for
              proteins, one pot of grain, one board of vegetables. Cooking the same dishes on five
              separate evenings repeats every one of those steps.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-[var(--color-cream)] rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-3 text-[var(--text-primary)]">
              The Limit Is Storage, Not Cooking
            </h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed">
              Cooked meat, stews and casseroles keep three to four days in a refrigerator held at
              40&nbsp;&deg;F or below. A five-day plan built entirely from fresh-cooked food is
              therefore one day past the published limit, which is why the planner asks which meals
              you intend to freeze.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>


      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Cost, storage life and reheating -->
  <section class="py-16 bg-[var(--color-cream)]">
    <div class="container max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="text-display text-2xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        What a Week of Meal Prep Costs, and How Long It Keeps
      </h2>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Two numbers decide whether batch cooking is worth the Sunday afternoon: the cost of a portion
        and the number of days the food stays good. Both are published figures rather than rules of
        thumb. The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics collects average prices for the ingredients, and the
        USDA publishes how long cooked food can sit in a refrigerator. This section puts the two
        together, then covers the step that spoils most meal-prep batches: cooling.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        From a price list to a cost per portion
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The prices below are BLS average prices for U.S. cities in September 2026, with the change from
        September 2025 calculated from the same series. They are averages across package sizes and
        regions, so treat them as a baseline rather than a quote from your own store. What they are good
        for is the arithmetic: convert the per-pound price to a per-kilogram price, multiply by the
        weight a portion uses, and add the components.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] border-collapse text-left text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
          <caption class="px-4 pb-2 text-left text-sm font-medium text-[var(--text-muted)]">
            BLS average prices, U.S. city average, September 2026, against September 2025.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">Item</th>
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">September 2026</th>
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">Change vs 2025</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Chicken breast, boneless</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$4.203 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">+0.7%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Ground beef, 100% beef</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$6.792 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">+7.4%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Rice, white, long grain, uncooked</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$1.118 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">+5.5%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Eggs, grade A, large</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$2.272 per dozen</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">&minus;34.9%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Milk, whole, fortified</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$4.106 per gallon</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">&minus;0.6%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Butter, in sticks</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$3.959 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">&minus;17.3%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Potatoes, white</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$0.989 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">&minus;2.5%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Tomatoes, field grown</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$1.992 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">+4.3%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Bananas</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$0.656 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">&minus;2.1%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Flour, white, all purpose</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">$0.545 per lb</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">&minus;3.2%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        A worked example, so the method is visible. Chicken breast at $4.203 per pound is $9.27 per
        kilogram once the pound is converted, so a 150&nbsp;g portion costs about $1.39. Rice at $1.118
        per pound is $2.46 per kilogram, so 80&nbsp;g of dry rice is about $0.20 and cooks up to roughly
        240&nbsp;g. A 150&nbsp;g potato and 100&nbsp;g of tomato add about $0.33 and $0.44. That is a
        portion at roughly $2.35 before oil, seasoning or cheese, and five of them cost about $11.77.
        Whether that is a saving depends entirely on what you would otherwise have bought, which is the
        number worth checking against your own receipts rather than against a claim on a website.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Two line items move the total more than the rest. Eggs fell 34.9% year on year and butter
        17.3%, which makes an egg-based breakfast the cheapest protein in the batch this month. Ground
        beef rose 7.4%, so a chilli or ragu costs noticeably more per portion than the chicken tray
        beside it.
        <a href="/tools/grocery-list" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">The grocery list
        generator</a> totals a shopping trip at the same prices, and
        <a href="/articles/grocery-budget-meal-planning" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the
        grocery budget guide</a> covers the wider food-price picture and the USDA forecast behind it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        How long a prepped meal actually keeps
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        This is the constraint that decides the shape of the week. FoodSafety.gov publishes
        refrigerator limits for cooked food, and for the components that make up a prepped container
        they cluster tightly around three to four days at 40&nbsp;&deg;F or below. Freezer times are
        about quality, not safety: food held continuously at 0&nbsp;&deg;F or below keeps
        indefinitely, and the months listed are how long it still tastes right.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[720px] border-collapse text-left text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
          <caption class="px-4 pb-2 text-left text-sm font-medium text-[var(--text-muted)]">
            Refrigerator and freezer storage for typical meal-prep components, from the FoodSafety.gov
            cold food storage chart.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">Component</th>
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">Refrigerator, 40&nbsp;&deg;F</th>
              <th class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 font-display text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-[var(--text-primary)]">Freezer, 0&nbsp;&deg;F</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Cooked meat or poultry</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 4 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">2 to 6 months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Soups and stews</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 4 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">2 to 3 months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Casseroles containing eggs, after baking</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 4 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">2 to 3 months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Quiche with filling, after baking</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 5 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">2 to 3 months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Prepared salads: egg, chicken, ham, tuna, macaroni</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 4 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Does not freeze well</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Hard-cooked eggs</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">1 week</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Do not freeze</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Pizza, cooked</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 4 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">1 to 2 months</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-[var(--color-cream-soft)]">
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">Luncheon meat, opened or deli sliced</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">3 to 5 days</td>
              <td class="border-b border-[var(--color-cream-dark)] px-4 py-3 align-top">1 to 2 months</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Read the table as a planning rule: a Sunday session covers Sunday to Wednesday, and anything
        meant for Thursday or Friday should go into the freezer on Sunday rather than sit in the
        refrigerator waiting. That is why the planner above separates the meals you will freeze. The
        other workable shape is two shorter sessions, one at the start of the week and one midweek,
        which keeps every container inside the three-to-four-day window without freezing anything.
        <a href="/tools/recipe-scaler" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">The recipe
        scaler</a> sizes a recipe up to the number of portions you are cooking in one go, and
        <a href="/tools/pantry-helper" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the pantry
        helper</a> finds a use for what is already on the shelf before you shop again.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-primary)] mb-4">
        Cooling, reheating and the two-hour rule
      </h3>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Most spoiled batches are cooled badly rather than cooked badly. A deep pot of stew left on the
        hob spends hours passing through the range between 40&nbsp;&deg;F and 140&nbsp;&deg;F, where
        bacteria multiply fastest. Divide it into shallow containers, stand the container in an
        ice-water bath and stir, and refrigerate as soon as it stops steaming. Do not stack warm
        containers against each other in the refrigerator; the cold air cannot circulate and the whole
        shelf warms up.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-base leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Reheating has its own published minimum: 165&nbsp;&deg;F (73.9&nbsp;&deg;C) for leftovers and
        casseroles, measured rather than guessed. Stir partway through in a microwave so the hot and
        cold spots even out, and let a covered dish stand for a minute before eating. Reheat once. A
        portion that has been warmed, eaten from and put back in the refrigerator has been through the
        danger zone twice, and the two-hour limit applies to the total time out of refrigeration,
        including the time on the plate. Above 90&nbsp;&deg;F ambient that limit halves to one hour,
        which matters for a packed lunch left in a hot car.
        <a href="/tools/leftover-matcher" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">The leftover
        matcher</a> turns what survives into the next two dinners, and
        <a href="/tools/nutrition-calculator" class="text-[var(--color-wine)] underline">the nutrition
        calculator</a> checks that a container actually hits the macros it was planned for.
      </p>

      <p class="text-[var(--text-secondary)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Sources:
        <a href="https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/cold-food-storage-charts" rel="noopener" class="underline">FoodSafety.gov, &ldquo;Cold Food Storage Chart&rdquo; (reviewed 19 September 2023)</a>;
        <a href="https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart" rel="noopener" class="underline">USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, &ldquo;Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart&rdquo; (last updated 14 April 2025)</a>;
        <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/buy-store-serve-safe-food/serving-safe-buffets" rel="noopener" class="underline">U.S. Food and Drug Administration, &ldquo;Serving Up Safe Buffets&rdquo;</a>;
        BLS Average Price Data, APU series, U.S. city average, September 2026 and September 2025.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Related Tools -->
  <section class="py-12 bg-[var(--color-cream)]">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="text-display text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text-secondary)] mb-6 text-center">
